## M5. Archive construction and target §8 — commission §§4.7 and 4.8

### M5.0 Scope, authority, and Stage 2b routing

M5 pins every byte of the normalized migration archive and of target `DECISIONS.md` §8. The archive
filename is the M0.1 pin; no section of this manifest recomputes it and no seat may re-derive it.

**Scope adopted, superseding this section's own prior description.** The placeholder these bytes replace
described M5 as the thirteen wrapper records, the thirteen archive-index lines, and the six-row retired
register. Two further surfaces are taken into M5 by architect decision, because no other manifest section
claims them and neither of the described surfaces can be assembled without them:

1. **the archive preamble, including the `E038` displaced-prose preservation construction** — the archive
   file's only non-wrapper surface, and the only location in that file where displaced prose can live
   without being absorbed into a hashed wrapper body;
2. **target §8's structural introduction carrying `E053`** — the bytes that directly introduce the thirteen
   archive-index lines. The index lines cannot be placed without the paragraph that heads them, so
   excluding it would leave M5 pinning lines with no pinned surrounding bytes, which is a Stage 2b stop
   under M1.

**No Stage 2b routing changes.** Codex remains the sole producer of target `DECISIONS.md`, of the
normalized archive, and of the preservation snapshot, constructing each from the ratified manifest bytes
without semantic improvisation, under commission §§5.1, 5.4, 5.5, and 5.6. This seat authors text and pins
bytes; it writes neither file. Nothing in M5 grants Codex a wording, labelling, dating, or boundary choice,
and nothing in M5 takes production away from Codex.

**Surfaces still unpinned by any manifest section, recorded so the gap is registered rather than discovered
at assembly.** Target §3's introduction, header row, separator row, and declared-total line; and the §§4–7
section headings with their transition paragraphs. The 65 target §3 index rows are already pinned, one per
M4 record at item 11, so what is missing is the table's framing and the section furniture, not its content.
These are owed before ratification under commission §4.8 and are outside M5 and M6.

### M5.1 Archive file shape, wrapper order, and the separator convention

The normalized archive is assembled in one order and one shape:

1. the preamble of M5.2, in full, including the preserved `E038` slice;
2. the thirteen wrappers of M5.5 in **ascending source-byte order**, which is the order the records are
   numbered in below.

Each wrapper is written as: the `###` heading line, one blank line, the field list with every field on
exactly one physical line in the fixed archive order, one blank line, then the preserved body bytes
unaltered. No historical body is rewrapped, spell-corrected, indentation-normalized, or Markdown-tidied.
Codex slices only by the pinned offsets and verifies the pinned SHA-256 before using any body.

**Separator bytes are written outside the body**, are never part of the hashed historical body, and are
never counted in the pinned byte length. Each record pins its own separator at item 13. The partition is
ten records taking one LF, two taking two LFs, and one taking none; the two-LF cases are exactly the two
bodies whose final byte is not `0x0a`, and the none case is exactly the last wrapper, whose body's own
final newline is the file's final byte. Either non-zero case leaves exactly one blank line before the next
`###` heading.

**Consequence for the target reconcile checker, asserted per record and never generically.** The parser's
`body` for a wrapper is `text.slice(bodyStart, bodyEnd)` where `bodyEnd` is the next `### ` line start, so
the parser's body includes the separator bytes. The checker must assert, for each of the thirteen records:

1. `parsed body length == pinned source length + pinned separator length`, where separator length is 1, 2,
   or 0 exactly as pinned at that record's item 13;
2. the first `pinned source length` bytes hash to that record's pinned SHA-256;
3. the remaining bytes equal exactly one LF, two LFs, or nothing, matching that record's pinned value.

**A generic trailing-whitespace strip is forbidden.** It would let arbitrary whitespace normalization pass
as byte preservation, and it would silently accept a corrupted last wrapper, whose parser body should
already equal its source slice exactly.

### M5.2 Archive preamble — exact bytes

Placed before the first `###` wrapper heading. The slice directive on the line reading
`<<E038-PRESERVED-SLICE>>` is resolved by M5.3 and is a pinned byte range with a pinned hash, not a
placeholder: Codex has a deterministic procedure that yields exact bytes and a hash to verify them
against, which is the same construction the thirteen wrapper bodies use.

```markdown
# DECISIONS archive — 2026-08-11 cleanup migration

Material condensed out of `DECISIONS.md` during the 2026-08-11 target-grammar migration. Each
wrapper below carries a migration-authored metadata header followed by a historical body preserved
byte-for-byte from `DECISIONS.md` at `MIGRATION_BASELINE`. Nothing in this file is current authority.
`Archive/DECISIONS-ARCHIVE-2026-07-14.md` remains the record of the earlier 2026-07-14
architectural-constitution pass and is not superseded by this file.

## Preserved displaced prose

This section carries prose condensed out of an entry that remains live under the same identity. It is not
an archive wrapper: it carries no metadata fields, produces no retired-register row, is reachable from no
archive-index line, and is pointed at only by the `Evidence` field of the live entry named below.

**Current producer assignment callout, displaced from the live standing invariant "Producer assignments
are operational state, not constitutional text" (`DECISIONS.md` §5 at `MIGRATION_BASELINE`).** The
paragraph below is preserved verbatim, including its self-reference: "this file" in it means
`DECISIONS.md` as it stood at the baseline, not this archive.

<<E038-PRESERVED-SLICE>>

## Archived entries
```

**The snapshot filename does not move.** `Archive/DECISIONS-PRE-MIGRATION-2026-07-29.md` is a ratified
literal in Amendment 4 §3.3 item 1 and taxonomy §9. It is not re-dated when the migration date moves, and
neither is the prior archive named in the preamble. The two archive filenames in this manifest therefore
behave differently on purpose, and a uniform re-date would break a ratified byte.

**Why this location and no other, verified against `lib/decisions-format.ts`.** `parseArchiveDocument`
segments the file on `^### ` headings only, and each wrapper body runs to the start of the next `### `
line or to end of file. Text placed after the last wrapper would be absorbed into that wrapper's body,
breaking its pinned byte and hash preservation; text placed between two wrappers would do the same to the
earlier one. Only text before the first `### ` heading sits outside every wrapper body, and it is never
parsed at all. A `##` heading does not segment the archive, so the preamble's two `##` headings are inert.

**Framing bytes are migration-authored and outside the preserved slice.** The bold lead-in paragraph above
is authored text and must never be counted inside the hashed slice. The target reconcile checker compares
the pinned 562 bytes against the file content between that framing paragraph and the `## Archived entries`
heading, exclusive of the blank lines separating them.

### M5.3 `E038` displaced-prose preservation construction

**Requirement discharged.** The phase-1 closure ruling
(`DECISIONS-CLEANUP-PHASE-1-CLOSURE-CODEX-WORK-ORDER-2026-07-28.md` §3 item 5) requires the displaced
dated assignment prose to be preserved verbatim in the phase-2 archive and pointed at from `E038`'s
evidence pointer, and states that this creates no archive-index row. Amendment 4 permits no fourteenth
wrapper for it, because `E038` remains live under the same title.

| pin | value |
|---|---|
| Source | `git show d499cc1d0916e03830489ec9cd0324cd1a203a73:DECISIONS.md` |
| Span | `[52641,53203)`, zero-based half-open |
| Byte length | `562` |
| SHA-256 | `d5603bebbc00308c04f938279960c2873a849ae1c622419a7095c0bf3789c3cf` |
| Final byte is `0x0a` | yes |
| Placement | inside the M5.2 preamble, before the first `###` heading |
| Archive-index line | none |
| Retired-register row | none |

**Span distinction, resolved.** The live-source packet's `E038` anchor is `[52641,53204)` and includes the
following blank line at `[53203,53204)`. The preservation slice deliberately excludes that byte, so the
slice ends at its own terminating newline and the archive supplies its own blank line. This is a
construction choice, not an unresolved discrepancy, and no preservation-boundary finding remains open.

**The `Evidence` pointer.** `E038`'s `Evidence` field at M4.45 names the normalized archive, which is a
Stage 2b output and therefore cannot be verified as tracked at the Stage 2a review commit. That is the
narrow sequencing exception ratified as Clause A of
`DECISIONS-MIGRATION-COMMISSION-AMENDMENT-1-2026-07-29.md`, which is exhausted by this single use. It is
the only path in the governed field-path population exempt from the `git ls-files` check, and M6.4 records
it as such rather than counting it tracked.

### M5.4 Target §8 structural introduction — `E053`

`E053` becomes structural prose under Amendment 4 §6. It receives no wrapper, no archive-index line, and no
register row, and it is the single place all three preservation files are named. It is the one structural
target-§8 row in the M0.3 accounting.

```markdown
## 8. Archive and retired identifiers

This section carries pointers only: no entry bodies and no archive wrappers. Condensed forcing-incident
narrative, superseded rulings, and lapsed lane contracts are preserved rather than deleted, across three
files. `Archive/DECISIONS-ARCHIVE-2026-07-14.md` remains the record of the 2026-07-14
architectural-constitution pass and is not edited by this migration. `Archive/DECISIONS-ARCHIVE-2026-08-11.md`
is the normalized archive for this migration and is the sole file every archive-index line below points
at. `Archive/DECISIONS-PRE-MIGRATION-2026-07-29.md` is a one-time byte-identical snapshot of this file at
`MIGRATION_BASELINE`, authorized once under taxonomy §9 Amendment 4; it is never current authority,
carries no wrapper, receives no index line, and is never parsed by the conformance checker.

Archive index — thirteen wrappers, one line each, in ascending source-byte order.
```

Parse note, load-bearing. No line in this prose opens `- **`, so `parseArchiveIndexLines` cannot capture
any of it as an index line and cannot emit `INVALID_FIELD_VALUE` for a missing pointer. The paragraph names
three files, but only the thirteen index lines contribute to the archive-source set that
`selectDefinitionIndex` counts, so `TARGET_ARCHIVE_SOURCE_COUNT` sees exactly one file. That set is built
from `parsed.archiveIndex` pointer files alone and is not influenced by prose or by any `Evidence` value.

### M5.5 Record template for the thirteen wrappers

Every record pins the twelve items of commission §4.7 under the fixed numbering below, plus one
manifest-added item 13. Item 13 is not a commission §4.7 item; it is pinned per record because the
separator length is load-bearing for the per-record preservation assertion of M5.1 and cannot be recovered
from the body hash.

Every wrapper's live metadata is `Kind: X`, `Status: SUPERSEDED`, `Force: HISTORICAL`. `Original Kind` and
`Original Status` are historical metadata, are never `X`, and are never scaffold shorthand. `Origin` takes
the exact shape `` `DECISIONS.md` <section> at `MIGRATION_BASELINE` ``, which is what the parser's `Origin`
regex requires. `Retired ID` appears if and only if the wrapper is ID-addressed.

The four ID-addressed wrappers carry `Date: 2026-07-28`, the historical retirement date ratified on that
day. The nine name-addressed wrappers carry the archival date, which is the M0.1 archive-filename value.
The asymmetry is deliberate: a retirement is dated when it happened and does not move.

