## M6. Optional-field omission register — commission §§4.4 item 10 and 4.6

Commission §4.6 requires a **manifest-level omission register** carrying, for every omitted `Evidence` or
`Owner` field, the source entry, the candidate label, and the disposition. That is §4.6's whole population:
§4.6 is the Evidence-and-Owner resolution section, and its "every omission" means every omission of those
two fields. §M6.3 is that register.

Two further instruments justify the wider five-field build. Commission §4.4 item 10 requires an explicit
`OMIT` for every absent optional field, and commission §10 requires an *optional-field* omission-register
summary in the migration receipt. §§M6.2 and M6.6 carry that wider population, which §4.6 does not itself
require and does not forbid.

The register is review evidence. **No byte of this section reaches `DECISIONS.md`,** and commission §4.6
says so explicitly. Nothing here authorizes a field, changes a field, or reopens a record.

### M6.0 Scope, authority, and reading rules

**Division of labour, stated so neither obligation is read onto the other surface.** Commission §4.4 item
10 requires each record to carry an explicit `OMIT` for every absent optional field — a *list*, and each
record carries one. Commission §4.6 item 4 requires the *reason* for an `Evidence` or `Owner` omission to
be stated "in the manifest's review note," which is this section. Most records volunteered their reason at
item 10 as well; ten did not, and their grounds are stated here and flagged at §M6.7. **No record required
a byte change to discharge §4.6,** because §4.6 never located the reason at the record.

**The field-test repair of 2026-08-04.** As first authored, this section's ground vocabulary collapsed the
`Evidence` and `Owner` eligibility tests into one complete-statement test and cited standing ruling 23 as
its authority. Ruling 23 says the opposite: it refines the complete-statement language **for `Evidence`
only** and leaves `Owner`'s whole-statement test intact. M2's target §1 bytes already record the
single-test formulation as a legacy-prose defect, repaired 2026-07-31. The vocabulary at §M6.1 is now
field-differentiated, ten `Evidence` rows were re-adjudicated on Evidence-appropriate grounds, and a
separate bounded repair to the item-10 `Evidence` reasoning of fifteen M4 records is governed by its own
work order. **No target byte, field line, statement, index row, date, or source span changed in that
repair.** The two governing tests are:

- **`Owner`** names the one tracked path that owns the whole live statement. It is defeated by an operative
  limb with its own independent enforcement surface in another tracked path — standing ruling 28 — and not
  by a clause that has no enforcement surface anywhere.
- **`Evidence`** names the one tracked source carrying the evidence, measurement, provenance, or method the
  statement is forbidden to restate. It need not support every limb, and it may not contradict or
  materially misrepresent any limb the statement keeps.

**Closed-world.** Part D §10.2 directs that the assembly pass inline the union of the Part A, Part B, and
Part C candidate registers rather than point at sibling draft files. This section does that. It cites no
draft as authority.

**Supersession.** This section supersedes Part A §4, Part B §5, Part C §7, and Part D §§10.1–10.2 as
construction authority. Those four registers remain historical inputs and are stale in the places recorded
at §M6.8. Where a draft register and this section differ, this section governs, and where this section and
an M4 record differ, **the M4 record governs and the difference is a defect to be reported, not resolved
here.**

**Derivation.** The ledger at §M6.2 and the register at §M6.3 were derived from the M4 records' own item 9
field lines and item 10 omission lists in this file, not from the drafts and not from memory. Candidate
labels that the M4 records do not name are carried forward from the draft registers, which are the record
of what was actually considered; a candidate is not invented here.

**Pointer convention, and why no reason is reproduced.** Each register row names the record that owns the
full reasoning. Reproducing that reasoning here would create a second source of truth for it and would let
the two drift, which is the defect class this migration exists to prevent. The `ground` column is a
**classification** of the reason, not a restatement of it.

**Date surfaces.** Candidate labels include repository paths whose filenames carry fixed ISO dates. Those
spans are date-bearing surfaces under M7.1 and the post-assembly derived report must claim each of them;
they are **not** functions of `MIGRATION_DATE` and re-render on no rebinding. Under standing ruling 30 this
section reproduces no migration-date literal: `E038`'s `Evidence` value is referred to at §M6.4 by pointer
to the M0.1 pin rather than typed out, so M6 adds no date-dependent span to the manifest.

### M6.1 Ground vocabulary

Fifteen grounds are used. A row may carry more than one. **Every ground declares which field it may serve.**
A vocabulary that cannot express that distinction is what allowed the two tests to be collapsed in the first
place, so the `field` column is load-bearing rather than decorative: an `Evidence` row carrying an
Owner-only ground, or the reverse, is a defect detectable mechanically.

The vocabulary also exists so that a reviewer can test a ground **as a class** across every row that claims
it, rather than adjudicating one hundred and ten calls separately.

| ground | field | meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `NO-CANDIDATE` | both | Nothing eligible was ever proposed for the field, at the record or in any draft register. |
| `NOT-A-PATH` | both | The candidate is an entry identity, a symbol, a directory, a command, or a concept, and is ineligible under the one-backticked-path grammar. This grammar constrains both fields alike. |
| `NOT-AN-AUTHORITY` | both | The candidate is a tracked file, but is an implementation script, artifact, data file, or operational record rather than a source carrying evidence or a path owning the rule. |
| `ARCHIVE-ONLY` | both | The substance lives only in material that is no tracked repository path — archived narrative, pre-compression governance text, or a dated work artifact. Location under `Archive/` is not itself disqualifying: `R3#0` and `R4#0` both carry `Archive/` paths as `Evidence`. |
| `UNRESOLVED-SUBJECT` | both | The entry's governing subject is an open question, so no source can be evidence for it and no path executes it. |
| `NO-EXECUTABLE-OWNER` | `Owner` only | The entry decides a practice, disposition, or scope boundary that no path executes. |
| `NO-SINGLE-OWNER` | `Owner` only | Distinct tracked paths independently enforce distinct operative limbs, so no one path owns the rule. The one-path grammar admits no concatenation. |
| `PARTIAL-OWNERSHIP` | `Owner` only | One candidate owns some operative limbs, but another operative limb has its own independent enforcement surface elsewhere. Standing rulings 11 and 28. A candidate is **not** disqualified merely because it cannot enforce a rationale, classification, or explanatory clause. |
| `CARRIED-ELSEWHERE` | `Owner` only | The candidate path is deliberately carried as a field on a different target block. |
| `PENDING` | `Owner` only | Implementation has not occurred, so naming a path would assert an execution that has not happened. |
| `SUPERSEDED-SCOPE` | `Owner` only | The candidate is scoped to behaviour or geometry that a later ratified entry supersedes. |
| `NO-COMPRESSED-SUBSTANCE` | `Evidence` only | The statement restates no measurement, count, provenance, method, or chronology that a separate tracked source owns, so the field has nothing to hold. |
| `CLAUSE-SCOPED` | `Evidence` only | The source citation was expressly attached to a different or omitted clause, so promoting it to entry-level `Evidence` would falsely widen its asserted scope. Standing ruling 19. This does **not** mean "supports only part of the statement": ruling 23 permits `Evidence` to support only the compressed-out evidentiary substance. |
| `NO-SINGLE-EVIDENCE-SOURCE` | `Evidence` only | The relevant provenance or evidence unit is inseparably divided across coequal sources, and choosing one would materially misrepresent it. The mere existence of multiple sources is not enough. |
| `WRONG-AUTHORITY` | `Evidence` only | The candidate is tracked but contradicts or materially misrepresents a limb the statement keeps. |

