**Two classes carry most of the register and are the ones to test as classes.** `NO-SINGLE-OWNER` at 19 rows
and `PARTIAL-OWNERSHIP` at 9 are the same whole-statement ownership test at different candidate counts, and
together they carry 28 rows; if that test is misapplied, it is misapplied 28 times. `NO-COMPRESSED-SUBSTANCE`
at 20 rows concentrates in the name-addressed `I` batches, where a single mistaken reading of what `Evidence`
is for would repeat across the whole run.

**No `Evidence` row carries an `Owner`-only ground and no `Owner` row carries an `Evidence`-only ground.**
That is the field-applicability invariant, and it is checkable mechanically against §M6.1's `field` column
rather than by reading.

---

## M7. Date-surface inventory — owner-directed candidate model

Amendment 1 Clause B §2.2 requires this inventory to assign a stable surface ID to every surface whose bytes
are a function of `MIGRATION_DATE`, and to every date-bearing surface that is **not** a function of it with
the reason each is fixed, using concrete locators rather than surface classes.

The rows themselves are authored after assembly, because they cite concrete locators in bytes that do not
yet exist. The **model** those rows must follow is fixed now, by owner direction of 2026-07-30, and
supersedes the provisional `D1`–`D5` / `F1`–`F8` table at Part D §8.1.

**Status.** This model governs candidate assembly and is binding on how the inventory rows are built. It is
**not** a separate ratification act, and no such act has occurred. Like every other byte in this file, it
remains subject to the exact-byte manifest ratification required by commission §12. Superseding a
provisional draft table is not the same as being ratified, and this section does not claim otherwise.

### M7.1 Occurrence definition

**One occurrence is one maximal ISO `YYYY-MM-DD` token span.** Maximal means the span is not extended by an
adjacent digit or hyphen that would make it part of a longer token, and is not subdivided into year, month,
or day components. Enclosing syntax — a filename, a backticked path, a field value, a heading, a prose
clause — is context that determines which surface the span belongs to; it is never itself the occurrence.

**Every span carries exactly one unique concrete surface ID.** No span is unmapped and no span is mapped
twice, which are two of the four proofs Clause B §2.2 requires of the derived report.

### M7.2 The overlap this model repairs

Part D §8.1's `D1` read "normalized archive filename, every occurrence," and its `D5` read "E038 `Evidence`
value." Those overlap: E038's `Evidence` value **is** an occurrence of the archive filename, so its single
date span satisfied two row definitions at once. Under a bijective mapping requirement that is not a
cosmetic redundancy — it is a span that would be counted twice or contested between rows.

**Resolution:** the E038 `Evidence` span is assigned to the `D5` family and is **excluded** from `D1`. `D1`
covers archive-filename occurrences other than that one. The same exclusion discipline applies to any future
collision discovered during the census: the more specific family claims the span, and the general family
states the exclusion explicitly rather than leaving it implied.

### M7.2a The gap this model repairs — family `D6`

Part D §8.1's `D1`–`D5` were all derived from **target-document** surfaces: the archive filename, wrapper
`Date` fields, archive-index phrases, the archive preamble, and `E038`'s `Evidence`. None of them reaches a
date-dependent surface that lives in **this manifest's own prose**.

One such surface exists. The manifest header's `MIGRATION_DATE` binding declaration carries a **bare**
date token, not a filename. `D1` covers normalized-archive-filename occurrences other than the more
specifically assigned `D5` occurrence, so `D1` does not claim a span that is not inside a filename, and no
other family claimed it either. Under M7.4 an unclaimed token is a finding returned to this seat for
disposition, never silently assigned and never defaulted to fixed on the grounds that it is not obviously
dependent.

**Disposition, by owner direction of 2026-07-31:** a distinct date-dependent family claims that span.

> **`D6` — manifest header `MIGRATION_DATE` binding declaration.**

`D6` claims the bound-value span only. The **owner-act date** in the same declaration, and the date inside
the binding record's filename on the following line, are separate maximal spans, are fixed, and are not
`D6`; an owner act is dated when it happened and does not move when the value it bound moves. This
exclusion is stated rather than implied, for the reason M7.2 gives.

`D1` and `D5` are unchanged. Like every other family here, `D6` is a **family definition only**; the
completed-manifest census expands it into concrete occurrence rows with their own IDs, locators, and byte
offsets, and no occurrence count is hand-authored at family level.

The generalizable point is M7.4's, sharpened: the census population is every ISO date token in the
completed manifest, including tokens in the manifest's own governance prose. A family set derived only
from target-document surfaces will under-cover that population.

### M7.3 Families, not rows

`D1`–`D5` and `F1`–`F8` are **families**, not final inventory rows. Each family expands into as many concrete
rows as it has spans, each with its own unique ID and its own locator and byte offset. A family with nine
spans yields nine rows, not one row asserting nine. A family-level table does not satisfy Clause B, and a
count asserted at family level is the hand-count defect class this workstream has already paid for.

### M7.4 The fixed census is open, not closed

`F1`–`F8` enumerate the fixed date-bearing surfaces Part D happened to identify. That is not a proven
complete population. Clause B's fourth proof — every surface the inventory marks fixed is byte-identical
across a rebinding — is only as strong as the census behind it.

**Therefore the census covers every ISO date token in the completed manifest, not only those named in Part
D.** Any token found that no `D` or `F` family claims is a finding returned to this seat for disposition,
never silently assigned, and never defaulted to fixed on the grounds that it is not obviously dependent.

### M7.5 Sequence

The derived occurrence report is a separate commission, not yet issued. Its order is fixed:

1. this seat completes the candidate bytes;
2. Codex censuses every maximal ISO date-token span in the candidate, reporting spans and locators with no
   surface assignment;
3. this seat maps each span to exactly one concrete surface ID within a family;
4. Codex validates exclusivity and coverage against the mapping and generates the report;
5. this seat inserts the exact generated bytes;
6. Codex re-derives over the completed file and proves the embedded report equals the regenerated report.

The report is placed last in this manifest so that inserting it cannot shift any earlier byte locator, and
step 6 scans the completed file rather than the pre-report candidate. Report rows carry occurrence IDs,
family IDs, concrete semantic locators, byte spans, and dependent/fixed dispositions — and **no literal
dates, no archive filenames, and no quoted date-bearing snippets**, so that the report cannot become its own
occurrence population.

Codex does not edit this manifest at any step. This seat owns every byte; the owner ratifies the final hash.

